Problem:
Find $a+b+c$, given that $x+y\neq -1$ and  \begin{align*}
ax+by+c&=x+7,\\
a+bx+cy&=2x+6y,\\
ay+b+cx&=4x+y.
\end{align*}

Solution:
answer:  We can add the first two equations to eliminate $a$ and $c$:

\begin{align*}
ax+by+c&=x+7,\\
a+bx+cy&=2x+6y.
\end{align*}

Adding these equations, we get:

\begin{align*}
ax+by+c+a+bx+cy&=x+7+2x+6y,\\
2a+2b+2c&=3x+7y.
\end{align*}

Next, we subtract the third equation from this result:

\begin{align*}
2a+2b+2c-(ay+b+cx)&=(3x+7y)-(4x+y),\\
2a+2b+2c-ay-b-cx&=3x+7y-4x-y,\\
a+b+c&=3x+6y.
\end{align*}

Since $x+y\neq -1$, we can divide both sides by $x+y$:

\begin{align*}
a+b+c&=3(x+y),\\
a+b+c&=\boxed{3}.
\end{align*}

The final answer is $3$. I hope it is correct.
