Problem:
A worker receives an annual wage of $\$20{,}000$, which he always deposits into a savings account at the end of the year. By the end of the third year (when he makes the third deposit), he wants to have at least $\$66,200$ in the account to finance the purchase of a house. What is the minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide? Express your answer as a percentage, but do not include the percent sign.

Solution:
answer:  Let $r$ be the annual interest rate. At the end of the first year, the worker has $20000(1+r)$ dollars in the account. At the end of the second year, he has $20000(1+r)^2$ dollars in the account. At the end of the third year, he has $20000(1+r)^3$ dollars in the account. We want this to be at least $66200$, so we have

$$20000(1+r)^3 \ge 66200.$$

Dividing both sides by $20000$, we get

$$(1+r)^3 \ge 3.31.$$

Taking the cube root of both sides, we get

$$1+r \ge \sqrt[3]{3.31} \approx 1.34.$$

Therefore, $r \ge 0.34$ or $34\%$. The minimal compound interest rate that the savings account must provide is $\boxed{34}$.

Final Answer: The final answer is $34$. I hope it is correct.
